{% set e = 'number.ember_satellite_mic_gain' %}{% set mn = state_attr(e,'min') | float(0) %}{% set mx = state_attr(e,'max') | float(42) %}{% set st = state_attr(e,'step') | float(6) %}{% set n = ((((mx - mn) / st) | round(0) | int) + 1) if st > 0 else 0 %}{% set g = (states(e) | float(-1)) | round(0) | int %}
{% for k in range(n) %}{% set s = (mn + k * st) | round(0) | int %}{{ '**' ~ s ~ '**' if s == g else '`' ~ s ~ '`' }}{{ ' · ' if not loop.last else ' dB' }}{% endfor %}

{% set cd = states('sensor.ember_satellite_mic_gain_codec') %}{% set ds = states('binary_sensor.ember_satellite_mic_gain_desync') %}{% set na = ['unknown','unavailable','none',''] %}{% if ds == 'on' %}⚠ **The row above is not what the chip holds** — REG16 reads **{{ cd | float(0) | round(0) | int }} dB**.{% elif ds == 'off' and cd not in na %}Codec holds **{{ cd | float(0) | round(0) | int }} dB** — *the last successful read-back agreed.*{% else %}Codec read-back **unavailable**, so agreement is *not confirmed*. A failed I2C read leaves these sensors untouched rather than reporting a false mismatch — this is silence, not assent.{% endif %}

{% if n != 8 %}⚠ **This control now reports {{ n }} stops, not eight.** The es8311's ADC PGA is a 3-bit field with exactly eight legal values, so either the firmware's range has drifted from the hardware or the driver changed. The row above is drawn from the entity's own `min`/`max`/`step`, so it is right; the note below assumes eight and should be treated as stale until someone checks.

{% endif %}*Eight stops, and only eight.* The es8311's ADC PGA is a 3-bit field, so nothing between these is representable — the control steps 6 dB because the hardware does. The device's own gain row draws eight stones where volume draws twenty, for the same reason.

**36** is deliberately below the top. ESPHome's own default is **42**, the maximum, and stacked on the pipeline's auto-gain it clips and pins the level gauges. Higher is *more sensitive*, not better.

Tune it against **Mic RMS** and **Peak** in this column — but only *mid-conversation*: `sound_level` is passive, so it reads while something else holds the microphone open and parks the rest of the time. A Peak pinned at the top of its scale is the symptom at 42.

